[Event "Dutch: Leningrad"]

{The Dutch (1...f5) grabs e4 and aims for a kingside attack. The Leningrad combines it with a g7 fianchetto.}
1. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
1... f5 {[%csl Ge4] The Dutch. Supports the centre: the pawn on f5 controls e4 and g4.}
2. g3 {Fianchetto. The pawn on g3 controls f4 and h4.}
2... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and d5.}
3. Bg2 {Long diagonal. Fianchettoes the bishop on g2, where it rakes the long diagonal through f3, e4 and d5.}
3... g6 {Fianchetto. The pawn on g6 controls h5 and supports the pawn on f5.}
4. Nf3 {Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
4... Bg7 {Fianchettoes the bishop on g7, where it rakes the long diagonal through f6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
5... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6. c4 {Space. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
6... d6 {Prepares ...e5. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
7. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
7... Qe8 {Prepares ...e5 and ...Qh5. The queen goes to e8.}
 (7... c6 {Flexible: prepares ...Qa5 or ...Na6. Supports the centre: the pawn on c6 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
8. d5 {Gains space. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls e6 and eyes the pawn on c6. Black's last move put a pawn on c6, which this now pressures.}
)
8. d5 {Gains space. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls c6 and e6.}
8... a5 {Stops b4. Gains space on the queenside; the pawn on a5 takes b4 away from the opponent.} *

[Event "Dutch: Anti-Dutch lines"]

{2.Bg5, 2.Nc3, 2.e4 and the London.}
1. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
1... f5 {Dutch. Supports the centre: the pawn on f5 controls e4 and g4.}
2. Bf4 {The London. Develops the bishop to f4, where it controls e5.}
 (2. e4 {The Staunton Gambit. Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and eyes the pawn on f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
2... fxe4 {Takes the pawn on e4, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
3. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and eyes the pawn on e4. That pawn is currently undefended.}
3... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls d5 and supports the pawn on e4.}
4. Bg5 {Pins. Develops the bishop to g5, where it controls f4 and eyes the knight on f6. Black's last move put a knight on f6, which this now pressures.}
4... g6 {Fianchetto. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
5. f3 {Opens lines. Supports the centre: the pawn on f3 controls g4 and eyes the pawn on e4.}
5... exf3 {Takes the pawn on f3; White will want to recapture.}
6. Nxf3 {Recaptures on f3, restoring the material balance.}
6... Bg7 {Fianchettoes the bishop on g7, where it rakes the long diagonal through f6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
)
 (2. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
2... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and d5.}
3. Bg5 {Pins. Develops the bishop to g5, where it controls f4 and eyes the knight on f6. Black's last move put a knight on f6, which this now pressures.}
3... d5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls c4 and e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8 and the queen on d8.}
4. Bxf6 {Takes the knight on f6; Black will want to recapture.}
4... exf6 {Recaptures on f6, restoring the material balance.}
5. e3 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e3 controls f4 and supports the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
5... c6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on c6 controls b5 and supports the pawn on d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
)
2... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and d5.}
3. e3 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e3 supports the pawn on d4 and the bishop on f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
3... g6 {Fianchetto. The pawn on g6 controls h5 and supports the pawn on f5.}
4. Nf3 {Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
4... Bg7 {Fianchettoes the bishop on g7, where it rakes the long diagonal through f6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5. h3 {Prepares Be2. The pawn on h3 controls g4.}
5... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.} *
